WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.500
A maintainable PowerShell script is built from understandable pieces.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:06.680
Cmdlets perform focused actions.

3
00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:09.580
Pipelines pass objects from one step to the next.

4
00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:12.080
Functions package reusable behavior.

5
00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:17.300
Modules group related functions into something that can be versioned, reviewed, and shared.

6
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.920
Parameters define the contract between the operator and the script.

7
00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:28.380
When those pieces are arranged clearly, future administrators can reason about the automation instead of treating it as a mysterious one-off file.

8
00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:31.940
Parameter design is one of the most important safety tools.

9
00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:35.400
Names should be consistent and specific.

10
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:38.780
Required parameters should be truly required.

11
00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:40.880
Defaults should be conservative.

12
00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:45.380
Inputs should be validated before the script reaches high-impact operations.

13
00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:52.440
A script that targets computers, users, groups, services, or paths should make the intended scope visible.

14
00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:57.260
Broad actions should require explicit choices rather than quiet assumptions.

15
00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:02.980
Comment-based help is useful because administration scripts often outlive the person who wrote them.

16
00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:15.240
Help text should explain the purpose of the script, its expected inputs, examples of normal use, what the script changes, what it only reports, and any permissions or operational conditions it expects.

17
00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:17.980
The goal is not to write a novel inside the file.

18
00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:23.680
The goal is to make the script understandable during a maintenance window, incident bridge, or handoff.

19
00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:30.040
Where appropriate, scripts should support preview behavior through PowerShell patterns such as WhatIf and Confirm.

20
00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:36.460
Those features help operators see intended changes before applying them and add friction before high-impact actions.

21
00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:43.600
They do not replace approval, testing, or authorization, but they do reduce the chance of accidental broad changes.

22
00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:49.620
A safe script is one that makes risky scope obvious and gives the operator a controlled path to proceed.
